  "theorem_name": "MesoscopicBufferWidthNecessaryForInverseLogReflectedMargin",
  "declared_proposition": "Let g_X be the ordered convolution of the prime indicator truncated at X. For every integer h with 0<=h<=X-2, g_X(2X-h)<=h+1. Therefore a normalized margin g_X(2X-h)/pi(X)>=c/log X forces h+1>=c*pi(X)/log X=(c+o(1))X/(log X)^2. In particular every buffer h=o(X/(log X)^2) is too thin for a uniform positive inverse-log margin, independently of any major/minor-arc estimate.",
  "mathematical_argument": "If p,q<=X and p+q=2X-h, then X-h<=p<=X. There are at most h+1 possible integers p, hence at most h+1 ordered prime pairs. Divide by pi(X) for the exact normalized ceiling. The stated necessary buffer scale follows from the prime number theorem pi(X)~X/log X. The finite audit checks the exact count and ceiling for three buffer widths at each of four cutoffs.",
